{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg2QDsf7RTrhvKctT4BgK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I updated `reference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md` (240 → 212 lines) and changed nothing else.\n\n1. **Day 14 entry:** \"Selective Memory\" was a 17 KB Objective-C ELF built against GNUstep's runtime. The config blob sat raw in its own `.hcfg` section at file offset 0xfa0 (key 0xa1, `BR14-000`, crc `0x79f491da`), so `has_hcfg_section` = `true`. The solver carried over from earlier days answered all 140 ids, and the Game Master (GM) marked it correct 9 s after start. Time Trial (2.42 s inside a 30 s window) and Mastery (3 sets, 420 ids, 6.01 s inside 30 s) were both correct on the first try. The `elf` / `x86-64` spellings were confirmed again, and the Objective-C classes/selectors theme in the prompt was never asked.\n2. **Lane sections:** The overview now says the new group always gets the same core ids plus one extra id from a small pool, and that the prompt's language theme hasn't been asked since day 4. I added an Objective-C row to \"Recognition cues -> first move\" and two pitfalls: chasing the prompt's theme, and timings flagged as slow that were really measured while the machine was busy. I compressed the day 1–13 entries to short lines and extended the conventions table to cover Objective-C and `has_hcfg_section` on day 14.\n3. **Variant axes:** Today's values went into the existing axes: config and key re-rolls (BR14-002/007/004), Time Trial having the same bytes as Mastery set 1, 3 Mastery sets, and `has_hcfg_section` for Objective-C (only a section literally named `.hcfg` counts, not the `__objc_*` ones). The blob's storage stayed the same across all of today's variants. I also added a speed-only axis: the \"4.11 s on single_solve\" flag was CPU contention, and the solver takes about 0.5 s when timed on its own."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_detail